# Changing Text and HTML Content

Now that we can get element objects, let's start modifying them. We begin with the content inside an element.

The first step is **always** the same: fetch a DOM element with `document.querySelector` or `querySelectorAll`. Then we read or set a property on the element object we got back.

## Reading and Writing `.textContent`

Every HTML element has an opening tag, some content, and a closing tag. The **`.textContent`** property is that content, as text.

```html id=page1
<p id="para">original text</p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const el = document.querySelector('#para');

console.log(el.textContent);

el.textContent = 'updated';

console.log(el.textContent);
```
```+id=page1
```

Reading `.textContent` gives us the text inside the element. Assigning to it replaces that text, and because we changed the DOM, the paragraph rendered above the output changed too.

## `.textContent` Versus `.innerHTML`

There is a second property, **`.innerHTML`**, which is the HTML content of the element rather than only its text. The difference shows up in four situations: reading each one, and writing each one.

### Reading

Take a paragraph with a nested element in it:

```html id=page2
<p id="demo">This is <strong>bold</strong> text</p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const el = document.querySelector('#demo');

console.log(el.textContent);
console.log(el.innerHTML);
```
```+id=page2
```

`.textContent` gives `This is bold text`. The `<strong>` element is ignored: we get the text from inside it, but no sign that it was formatted at all.

`.innerHTML` gives `This is <strong>bold</strong> text`, the actual markup that lives inside the element.

### Writing

Now the more surprising half. `.textContent` treats whatever you assign as **literal text**, and `.innerHTML` treats it as **HTML to parse**.

```{javascript autorun}
const el = document.querySelector('#demo');

el.textContent = 'new <strong>text</strong>';

console.log(el.innerHTML);
```
```+id=page2
```

Look at the rendered paragraph: it literally reads `new <strong>text</strong>`, angle brackets and all. Nothing became bold. Setting `.textContent` means "these are characters", so the browser replaces `<` and `>` with the special sequences `&lt;` and `&gt;`, which display as those characters rather than starting a tag. You can see that in the logged `innerHTML`.

Change one word and the behavior flips completely:

```{javascript autorun}
const el = document.querySelector('#demo');

el.innerHTML = 'new <strong>HTML</strong>';

console.log(el.textContent);
```
```+id=page2
```

Now `HTML` is genuinely bold, because the browser parsed the string and built a real `<strong>` element inside the paragraph.

To summarize all four:

- **Reading `.textContent`** returns the text only, with any inner tags stripped.
- **Reading `.innerHTML`** returns the markup, tags included.
- **Setting `.textContent`** escapes `<` and `>`, so the string appears as literal characters.
- **Setting `.innerHTML`** parses the string as HTML and builds real elements.

## Prefer `.textContent`

Whenever you only need to change text, use `.textContent`. Reach for `.innerHTML` only when you specifically need to insert structure, and only with strings you wrote yourself.

The reason is security. Because setting `.innerHTML` parses its string as HTML, any tags in that string become live elements. That is fine when you wrote the string. It is dangerous when the string came from a user.

Imagine a message board that appends whatever a visitor typed:

```javascript
const board = document.querySelector('#board');
const userMessage = getMessageFromUser();

board.innerHTML = board.innerHTML + userMessage;   // dangerous
```

If someone types `Hello everyone!`, fine. If they type this instead:

```html
<img src="x" onerror="alert('you have been hacked')">
```

then the browser parses it as HTML, creates a real `<img>` element, fails to load the image named `x`, and runs the `onerror` JavaScript, in the browser of every visitor who views that page. A real attack would take session cookies rather than show an alert. This class of attack is called **cross-site scripting**, or XSS.

The fix is to route anything from a user through `.textContent`, which escapes it:

```javascript
const board = document.querySelector('#board');
const userMessage = getMessageFromUser();

const line = document.createElement('p');
line.textContent = userMessage;   // escaped as text, never parsed as HTML
board.appendChild(line);
```

(`createElement` and `appendChild` are how we build new elements from scratch. We cover them properly in a couple of lessons.)

The rule of thumb: use `.textContent` by default, and use `.innerHTML` only with HTML you control.

## A Note on `.innerText`

You will run into a third property, `.innerText`, which is almost the same as `.textContent`. The differences are small and mostly about spacing and about elements that are hidden by CSS: `.innerText` accounts for how the browser will actually render the content, while `.textContent` does not.

In this specialization we will use `.textContent`. When you see `.innerText` elsewhere, it is doing nearly the same job.

## Exercises

1. Change the paragraph's text to exactly `Updated message`.

    ```html id=page3
    <p id="note">Original message</p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const message = document.querySelector('#note');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page3
    ```
    ```+solution
    const message = document.querySelector('#note');

    message.textContent = 'Updated message';
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#note').should('have.text', 'Updated message').run('Set the paragraph text to `Updated message`', 'Updated the paragraph text');
    ```

2. Put a real `<strong>` element containing the word `Important` inside the empty paragraph.

    ```html id=page4
    <p id="notice"></p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const notice = document.querySelector('#notice');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page4
    ```
    ```+solution
    const notice = document.querySelector('#notice');

    notice.innerHTML = '<strong>Important</strong>';
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#notice strong').should('have.text', 'Important').run('Add a real `strong` element containing `Important`. Setting `textContent` would show the tag as literal characters instead', 'Inserted a strong element');
    ```

3. Read both properties of the list below. Store its text in `listText` and its markup in `listMarkup`.

    ```html id=page5
    <ul><li>Milk</li><li>Apples</li></ul>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const list = document.querySelector('ul');

    const listText = /* Your code here */;
    const listMarkup = /* Your code here */;

    console.log(listText);
    console.log(listMarkup);
    ```
    ```+id=page5
    ```
    ```+solution
    const list = document.querySelector('ul');

    const listText = list.textContent;
    const listMarkup = list.innerHTML;

    console.log(listText);
    console.log(listMarkup);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(listText).should('equal', 'MilkApples').run('`listText` should be the list\'s `textContent`, which is the text with no tags', 'Read the text content');
    wrap(listMarkup).should('equal', '<li>Milk</li><li>Apples</li>').run('`listMarkup` should be the list\'s `innerHTML`, which includes the `li` tags', 'Read the HTML content');
    ```

